<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Additional ideas we explored that did not outperform</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted preliminary experimentation with several modifications to <span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Beyond the use of REINFORCE as <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Using REINFORCE as ℒ_𝑢 ‣ Appendix C Additional proofs ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>), we also tried:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Annealing the <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> that trades off <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> (from low to high or high to low)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Annealing <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})\propto p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})\propto p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}</annotation></semantics></math> (from low to high or high to low)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Including a reward transformation like <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{\prime}(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq r(\bm{s}_{0:T})-c\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq r(\bm{s}_{0:T})-c\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Reward transformation comparison ‣ Appendix C Additional proofs ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>) in <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a hyperparameter which may be different from the <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> used in <span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="A4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A4.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We found none of these to improve the Pareto frontier of <span id="A4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, we found in the setting in <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> that the final behavior of the model depended almost exclusively on the final value of <math id="A4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (assuming reasonable early values of <math id="A4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> that did not lead to degenerate policies), suggesting that early values of <math id="A4.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> are less important.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We speculate that annealing <math id="A4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> may be similar to tuning the learning rate, in that a lower learning rate for <math id="A4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> is similar to targeting lower values of <math id="A4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> earlier on in training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the reward transformation, since <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Reward transformation comparison ‣ Appendix C Additional proofs ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> already shows a connection between <span id="A4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> and transforming the reward, including an additional reward transformation could be seen as a sort of “double counting”, and does not help with the core advantage of <span id="A4.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span>, which is using <math id="A4.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce low-reward outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We found using <math id="A4.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]</annotation></semantics></math> to generally perform worse. We believe this is because <math id="A4.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq e^{-\beta r(\bm{s}_{0:T})}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a stronger gradient signal towards lower reward outputs, whereas <math id="A4.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq\mathbb{I}[r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta]</annotation></semantics></math> reweights outputs that satisfy the reward threshold purely based on <math id="A4.I3.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (and can fail to provide any signal if no drawn samples satisfy <math id="A4.I3.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;\eta</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.6 </span>Compute usage details</h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments with DistilGPT2 and with SmolLM-135M-Instruct were conducted on a single GPU, usually either an A40 or A6000 (48G memory). Each seed in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> took no longer than 30 minutes, while training SmolLM-135M-Instruct in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> took around 2 hours, with an additional <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>40 minutes for adversarial robustness evaluation (<a href="#S4.F6" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Experiments in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> that trained Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct were distributed over 4 L40S GPUs (48G memory each), taking a bit over 4 hours for each seed (<math id="A4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>16 GPU hours total). Subsequent adversarial robustness evaluation was done on a single L40S GPU and took <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>40 minutes per seed.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each method, we conducted a coarse grid search over hyperparameters, relying on heuristics and information gained from a smaller number of seeds to narrow the search space. Considering that many methods have multiple hyperparameters (<a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 More details on hyperparameters ‣ Appendix D Experiment details ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> for more details), and we also tried ideas and configurations that are not included in the main results (e.g., <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Additional ideas we explored that did not outperform ‣ Appendix D Experiment details ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>), the total compute usage is significantly greater.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional experiment results</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Additional results for <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we consider the same setting as in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> but with the addition of a KL penalty to the reward.
Though a KL penalty is not necessary for this setting, in practice it is common to include in the reward a KL to prior penalty to help stabilize results, preserve fluency, and mitigate reward hacking (<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Reinforcement learning ‣ 2 Background and preliminaries ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). This makes the new reward (return): <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{\prime}(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq r(\bm{s}_{0:T})-\frac{1}{\beta}D_{\textsc{kl}}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(p_{\theta}\,\middle\rvert\,p_{0}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">kl</mtext></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}(\bm{s}_{0:T})\coloneqq r(\bm{s}_{0:T})-\frac{1}{\beta}D_{\textsc{kl}}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(p_{\theta}\,\middle\rvert\,p_{0}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this setting, we choose a coefficient value of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\beta}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\beta}=10</annotation></semantics></math> across all methods. This is a high value, meant to demonstrate differences with the results in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, as our experiments with lower KL divergences (e.g., <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\beta}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\beta}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\beta}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\beta}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) showed essentially the same results as <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. The addition of the KL penalty may also better correspond to the experiments in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> which have KL penalties.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A5.F8" title="In E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F8" title="In E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show results, using the same evaluation as in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> except with return (reward including the KL penalty) for <a href="#A5.F8" title="In E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Together, they show that <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> achieves a favorable tradeoff compared to reward-transformed-REINFORCE, achieving lower probabilities of bad output for similar levels of average return.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F8.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/toyrlhf_kl10_10_18_final_logprobbad.svg" id="A5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F8.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A5.F8.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Toy experiment: number of samples drawn vs. log total probability of bad outputs based on analytic calculation on a list of bad words. <span id="A5.F8.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> achieves lower probabilities of bad output compared to reward-transformed-REINFORCE (compare the red line with short dashes to dash-dotted green line, and the purple dotted line to the blue line with long dashes) as training progresses.
Results are averaged over 3 seeds with 95% confidence intervals shown.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/toyrlhf_kl10_10_18_final_untransformed_ret.svg" id="A5.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F8.fig2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A5.F8.fig2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Toy experiment: Number of samples drawn vs. average return (including the KL penalty) as estimated from 500 samples. <span id="A5.F8.fig2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> achieves similar return to reward-transformed-REINFORCE (compare the red line with short dashes to dash-dotted green line, and the purple dotted line to the blue line with long dashes). Combined with <a href="#A5.F8" title="In E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, where <span id="A5.F8.fig2.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> achieves a lower probability of bad output, this demonstrates that <span id="A5.F8.fig2.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> achieves a better tradeoff. Results are averaged over the same 3 seeds as in <a href="#A5.F8" title="In E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> with 95% confidence intervals shown.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the inclusion of a non-zero KL penalty forces the existence of a tradeoff between expected return and the probability of bad outputs. For an informal proof sketch of this, there exists some optimal policy that achieves maximum expected return (and has the lowest probability of bad outputs among all policies that achieve maximum expected return). This policy has some non-zero probability of bad outputs (assuming the prior policy <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has some non-zero probability of bad outputs). Therefore, any policy that achieves lower probability of bad outputs must achieve lower return (otherwise that would be the optimal policy instead). Thus, in practical settings with a KL penalty, when we train for long enough to be near convergence/optimality, we should expect to suffer some reduction in average return if we wish to reduce the probability of bad outputs. Our goal is to improve this tradeoff/frontier.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Additional results for <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, baselines were provided twice the number of <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> updates to compensate for the additional compute <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> requires.
<a href="#A5.F10" title="In E.2 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F10" title="In E.2 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> show the same results as <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> except using the same number of <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> updates for all methods. The improvement of <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> over these baselines is greater in this case. This is expected, since additional optimization (more <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> updates) should improve the frontier for all methods (so long as they have not fully converged).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F10.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/len20_10_23_kl0_2_epi2_s10_sameepi_final_thr-5_frontier_ret.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F10.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><a href="#Thmsetting1" title="Setting 1: ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Setting</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="A5.F10.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Plot shows average return (including KL divergence) vs. total probability of bad outputs (<math id="A5.F10.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;-5</annotation></semantics></math>) estimated from <math id="A5.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, evaluated on 10,000 held-out prompts with 5 samples each.
Each point is an average over 10 seeds with 95% confidence intervals for both axes. <span id="A5.F10.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> clearly improves the frontier.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F10.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/1B_len100_10_23_kl2_sameepi_final_thr-7_frontier_ret.svg" id="A5.F10.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F10.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><a href="#Thmsetting2" title="Setting 2: ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Setting</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="A5.F10.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Plot shows average return (including KL divergence) vs. total probability of bad outputs (<math id="A5.F10.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;-7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\bm{s}_{0:T})&lt;-7</annotation></semantics></math>) estimated from <math id="A5.F10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, evaluated on 2,500 held-out prompts with 4 samples each.
Each point is an average over 5 seeds with 95% confidence intervals for both axes. <span id="A5.F10.fig2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> clearly improves the frontier.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A5.F12" title="In E.2 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F12" title="In E.2 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> show the same results as <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> except using CVaR as the metric on the y-axis. In this case, higher and to the right is better. Results are qualitatively similar; <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> improves on the Pareto frontier. For consistency, CVaR thresholds were chosen such that the reward of samples below the CVaR threshold was similar to those satisfying the thresholds in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F12.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/len20_10_23_kl0_2_epi2_s10_cvar_final_thr-5_frontier_ret.svg" id="A5.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F12.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><a href="#Thmsetting1" title="Setting 1: ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Setting</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="A5.F12.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Plot shows average return (including KL divergence) vs. CVaR at threshold 0.0001 (average return for the worst 0.01% of samples) estimated from <math id="A5.F12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, evaluated on 10,000 held-out prompts with 5 samples each.
Each point is an average over 10 seeds with 95% confidence intervals for both axes.
Similar to <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="A5.F12.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> improves the Pareto frontier at higher <math id="A5.F12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F12.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21184v1/1B_len100_10_23_kl2_cvar_final_thr-7_frontier_ret.svg" id="A5.F12.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F12.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><a href="#Thmsetting2" title="Setting 2: ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Setting</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="A5.F12.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Plot shows average return (including KL divergence) vs. CVaR at threshold 0.003 (average return for the worst 0.3% of samples) estimated from <math id="A5.F12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, evaluated on 2,500 held-out prompts with 4 samples each.
Each point is an average over 5 seeds with 95% confidence intervals for both axes.
Similar to <a href="#S4.F4" title="In Tradeoffs between average return and undesirable outputs ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="A5.F12.fig2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RePULSe</span> improves the Pareto frontier.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Experiment qualitative results</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">WARNING: this section may contain offensive or harmful language.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Qualitative results for <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Toy experiment – illustrating motivation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Near the end of training, typical outputs <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{s}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> include: “member member” or “contributor contributor” and typical outputs from <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> include: “member ass” or “contributor ass”. <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates on relatively neutral descriptors (with the repetition being a quirk of the toxicity model combined with no regularization to preserve fluency), while <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> finds more “toxic” outputs, as quantified by low reward from the toxicity classifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When adding a KL penalty, as in <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Additional results for ‣ Appendix E Additional experiment results ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>, outputs are much more diverse. For example, <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> samples include “good man”, “world leader”, “college professor”, while <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> samples include “massive prick”, “special asshole”, “big dick”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Qualitative results for <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below are some examples of outputs <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{s}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) and <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) given prompts <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> near the end of training, for <a href="#Thmsetting1" title="Setting 1: ‣ 4.3 More realistic experiments – investigating findings in practice ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Setting</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{longtblr}</span>
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">[
caption = Examples of prompts and outputs from <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}(\bm{s}_{1:T}|\bm{s}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (SmolLM-135M-Instruct). <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> learns to generally output low-reward sequences, which are outputs that we would not want an LM to produce. On adversarial/harmful prompts, this includes harmful responses. For non-adversarial prompts, this leans towards more nonsensical responses.
